1
00:00:00,990 --> 00:00:03,070
This lecture is Introduction to

2
00:00:03,070 --> 00:00:05,370
Facilitated Positional Release.

3
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:12,490
Our session objectives
are posted there on the

4
00:00:12,490 --> 00:00:16,570
slide. We're going to talk
about the history of FPR.

5
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:20,630
We'll explain the
principles of FPR, the

6
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:23,330
physiology, physiologic
mechanisms involved.

7
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:26,470
I'll talk about some indications
and contraindications

8
00:00:26,470 --> 00:00:29,430
of the FPR technique
and explain how to

9
00:00:29,430 --> 00:00:32,470
apply the principles of
FPR to treat any given

10
00:00:32,470 --> 00:00:34,590
traumatic dysfunction
based on your knowledge of

11
00:00:34,590 --> 00:00:36,630
anatomy and the function
of the given region.

12
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:41,370
FPR, or Facilitated
Physitional Release,

13
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:44,070
was developed by Dr.
Stanley Shiawitz.

14
00:00:44,270 --> 00:00:50,810
He was the dean of
NYITCOM, or when he

15
00:00:50,810 --> 00:00:55,190
was dean it was NICOM,
from 1991 to 2002.

16
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:59,270
The story goes that
90 seconds was too

17
00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:01,310
long for Dr. Shiawicz
to do Counterstrain.

18
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:04,480
He had a very busy
practice, and he had

19
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:07,870
to do everything as
quickly as possible.

20
00:01:09,170 --> 00:01:12,530
So he developed Facilitated
Positional Release,

21
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:16,790
and the students and
fellows he was working

22
00:01:16,790 --> 00:01:19,110
with at the time didn't
know what to call it,

23
00:01:19,110 --> 00:01:22,690
so they used to document
on the chart I.O.,

24
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:25,350
And when Dr. Scheiwitz
saw IO, he had

25
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:28,490
asked them, what
does IO stand for?

26
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:31,290
And they said it's
instant osteopathy.

27
00:01:31,390 --> 00:01:34,610
So he figured they needed
a better name than that.

28
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:37,450
So they eventually came up
with facilitated positional

29
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:40,960
release. But that's a
little back story on FPR.

30
00:01:43,570 --> 00:01:49,250
FPR is a passive,
indirect technique.

31
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:55,830
It's suggested that FPR
uses the muscle spindle

32
00:01:55,830 --> 00:01:58,670
reflex. The theory is that
compressing the muscles

33
00:01:58,670 --> 00:02:02,030
past their neutral position
results in a quieting

34
00:02:02,030 --> 00:02:05,190
of the gamma motor gain
to the muscle spindle and

35
00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:07,930
this leads to a resetting
of the tension and

36
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:11,210
resetting of the hypertonicity
of the muscle fibers.

37
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:16,370
So what are the basics
of performing FPR?

38
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,900
First you want to neutralize

39
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:20,390
the position of
the dysfunction.

40
00:02:21,490 --> 00:02:23,390
Then you want to
add a facilitating

41
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:25,470
force to the point
being treated

42
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:28,150
and a facilitating
point force can be

43
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:31,270
either compression,
traction, or torque.

44
00:02:32,050 --> 00:02:35,070
You want to maintain that
facilitating force and

45
00:02:35,070 --> 00:02:38,270
guide the dysfunction
into its freedoms and hold

46
00:02:38,270 --> 00:02:40,510
it in that position for
three to five seconds.

47
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:43,210
Then you release
the facilitating

48
00:02:43,210 --> 00:02:45,830
force and guide the
dysfunction back to

49
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:47,990
the baseline position
and reassess.

50
00:02:50,110 --> 00:02:51,610
Now we're going to
talk a little bit about

51
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:54,650
indications and
contraindications of FPR.

52
00:02:54,930 --> 00:02:58,590
Well, basically, any
condition where a

53
00:02:58,590 --> 00:03:01,530
somatic dysfunction is
present is an indication

54
00:03:01,530 --> 00:03:03,550
for facilitated
positional release.

55
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:07,290
Something to note is you
might want to treat the

56
00:03:07,290 --> 00:03:09,750
larger muscle groups
first before treating the

57
00:03:09,750 --> 00:03:12,750
joints, and this goes along
with what you might do

58
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:15,750
with other techniques as
well. If the paraspinal

59
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:17,650
muscles are really tight,
you're going to want to

60
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:20,030
treat those muscles before
you get down into the

61
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:24,750
individual thoracic or
lumbar or cervical segments.

62
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:29,890
So for example, someone with
neck pain due to whiplash,

63
00:03:29,890 --> 00:03:33,070
you'll treat that cervical
soft tissue first and then

64
00:03:33,070 --> 00:03:36,450
move on to the individual
cervical spinal segments.

65
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:41,570
So what are some
contraindications of FPR?

66
00:03:42,090 --> 00:03:43,950
Well, if a patient's
not able to

67
00:03:43,950 --> 00:03:45,410
tolerate treatment
position, you're not

68
00:03:45,410 --> 00:03:47,630
going to perform
FPR on this patient.

69
00:03:48,090 --> 00:03:51,370
Or what you might be
able to do is modify

70
00:03:51,370 --> 00:03:55,470
your technique to perform
FPR, but maybe not

71
00:03:55,470 --> 00:03:57,170
necessarily the way
you originally intended

72
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:59,890
to do it, or use a
different technique.

73
00:04:00,310 --> 00:04:03,150
If somebody can't tolerate
compression, maybe

74
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:05,170
you can add a little
traction. Something

75
00:04:05,170 --> 00:04:07,270
like that would be how
you would modify it.

76
00:04:08,470 --> 00:04:12,030
If a patient has radicular
pain when performing to the

77
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:14,730
cervical spine using
compression, similar to when

78
00:04:14,730 --> 00:04:17,690
you're doing a spiraling
maneuver, you're not going to

79
00:04:17,690 --> 00:04:21,630
want to do FPR using compression
in the cervical spine.

80
00:04:21,630 --> 00:04:24,050
So what do you do? Maybe
reposition, try it with

81
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:27,250
traction like I said earlier,
or try another technique.

82
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:32,190
Another contraindication
would be if the patient

83
00:04:32,190 --> 00:04:35,030
has any condition in
the region being treated

84
00:04:35,030 --> 00:04:37,930
where an introduction
of torque, traction,

85
00:04:37,930 --> 00:04:41,530
or compression is
contraindicated, or also the

86
00:04:41,530 --> 00:04:44,030
treatment position if
it's contraindicated. For

87
00:04:44,030 --> 00:04:46,970
instance, hip replacement.
You don't want to,

88
00:04:48,230 --> 00:04:50,830
if somebody had a hip
replacement, the treatment

89
00:04:50,830 --> 00:04:54,450
position for treating
a herniated disc might

90
00:04:54,450 --> 00:04:59,490
not be able to be
performed for this patient.

91
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:01,750
Someone with shoulder
pathology, the

92
00:05:01,750 --> 00:05:04,010
treatment position for
rib one, for example,

93
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:07,430
might not be tolerated
by the patient. A patient

94
00:05:07,430 --> 00:05:09,930
who has chronic shoulder
dislocation or separation,

95
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:11,830
any treatment that moves or

96
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:13,210
puts force into the shoulder

97
00:05:13,570 --> 00:05:16,770
might not be tolerated
by the patient or could

98
00:05:16,770 --> 00:05:20,050
possibly dislocate the
shoulder again. So you're not

99
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:22,990
going to perform the
technique if that's the case.

100
00:05:23,950 --> 00:05:27,370
Any recent trauma,
fracture, or dislocation

101
00:05:27,370 --> 00:05:29,830
at the area involved
with the treatment

102
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:34,090
would be a contraindication
of doing FPR.

103
00:05:34,570 --> 00:05:38,830
Also, you would want to use
caution in osteoporosis.

104
00:05:40,150 --> 00:05:43,470
If there's a malignancy,
you know, osteoporosis or

105
00:05:43,470 --> 00:05:46,750
malignancy, maybe putting
compression could possibly give

106
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:50,630
a compression fracture.
So you want to be careful.

107
00:05:50,810 --> 00:05:53,270
You shouldn't be putting
that much pressure,

108
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:55,750
that much compression
into an area.

109
00:05:55,750 --> 00:05:58,150
Even with someone
with osteoporosis, you

110
00:05:58,150 --> 00:05:59,750
should be able to
tolerate it, but it's

111
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:01,250
a caution, so you
have to be careful.

112
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:05,570
Certain rheumatologic
disorders, if the treatment

113
00:06:05,570 --> 00:06:10,770
position involves a
position that the patient

114
00:06:10,770 --> 00:06:13,910
can't tolerate, you want
to be careful. If there

115
00:06:13,910 --> 00:06:16,970
are any congenital
malformations, the same thing.

116
00:06:16,970 --> 00:06:20,010
If somebody has stenosis,
again, depending on what

117
00:06:20,010 --> 00:06:22,850
you're doing, you may be
exacerbating the pain.

118
00:06:23,290 --> 00:06:26,270
And, of course, always use
your clinical judgment for

119
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:29,250
any disease that the patient
has. If you think that

120
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:32,830
it's contraindicated, then
do something different.

121
00:06:34,510 --> 00:06:38,850
Side effects of FPR
are similar to most

122
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:41,850
manipulative techniques,
and they are transient.

123
00:06:41,850 --> 00:06:45,090
Fatigue, soreness,
stiffness. and this is

124
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:47,490
usually primarily due to
the restoration of the

125
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:51,150
circulation to the
previously dysfunctional area

126
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:54,090
and the release of
muscular waste products.

127
00:06:57,020 --> 00:06:58,730
Let's go through a case.

128
00:06:59,150 --> 00:07:02,450
A 29-year-old male with no
significant past medical

129
00:07:02,450 --> 00:07:04,390
history presents to
your office complaining

130
00:07:04,390 --> 00:07:08,310
of achy low back pain for
10 years. He was a gymnast

131
00:07:08,310 --> 00:07:10,710
in high school and has
had pain intermittently

132
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:13,870
since then. When his back
goes out, the severity

133
00:07:13,870 --> 00:07:16,770
of the pain is a sharp
8 out of 10. On a normal

134
00:07:16,770 --> 00:07:19,970
day, it is a 4 out of 10
at the end of the day.

135
00:07:20,090 --> 00:07:22,150
Nothing really makes
it better or worse.

136
00:07:23,330 --> 00:07:26,330
The pain is non
-radiating. He has no

137
00:07:26,330 --> 00:07:28,410
associated numbness,
tingling, weakness,

138
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:31,110
or bowel or bladder
function changes.

139
00:07:31,510 --> 00:07:37,530
He brought in to his
visit a recent lumbosacral

140
00:07:37,530 --> 00:07:40,770
spine x-ray and an MRI
which showed some mild

141
00:07:40,770 --> 00:07:44,110
degenerative changes but
nothing else of significance.

142
00:07:45,690 --> 00:07:49,990
He has had some childhood
asthma, no surgeries,

143
00:07:50,390 --> 00:07:53,910
he denies alcohol,
drug, or tobacco use.

144
00:07:55,190 --> 00:07:58,410
His family history,
both parents and his

145
00:07:58,410 --> 00:08:00,690
siblings, two siblings
are alive and well

146
00:08:01,030 --> 00:08:03,510
and the review systems is only

147
00:08:03,510 --> 00:08:04,970
positive for the back pain.

148
00:08:07,150 --> 00:08:09,770
On physical exam,
his lower extremity

149
00:08:09,770 --> 00:08:12,760
motor is 5 out
of 5 bilaterally.

150
00:08:13,150 --> 00:08:17,710
L4 and S1 reflexes are 2
out of 4 bilaterally. He has

151
00:08:17,710 --> 00:08:20,930
no gross sensory deficit in
his lower extremities. He

152
00:08:20,930 --> 00:08:23,730
has a negative straight
leg raise bilaterally, and

153
00:08:23,730 --> 00:08:27,990
he has no costovertebral
angle tenderness bilaterally.

154
00:08:29,650 --> 00:08:35,080
He has a negative Faber
test. You find paravertebral

155
00:08:35,130 --> 00:08:38,130
muscle spasm bilaterally.
He has bilateral

156
00:08:38,130 --> 00:08:41,510
psoas spasm. He has
bilateral hamstring spasm.

157
00:08:41,510 --> 00:08:45,870
You note a right quadratus
lumborum spasm, and you

158
00:08:45,870 --> 00:08:49,430
also find L5 extended
rotated side vent right.

159
00:08:52,590 --> 00:08:57,610
Your assessments are low
back pain, muscle spasm,

160
00:08:58,190 --> 00:09:01,210
somatic dysfunction
in the lumbar region,

161
00:09:01,210 --> 00:09:03,970
and somatic dysfunction
in the lower extremity.

162
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:07,410
So what are we
going to do here?

163
00:09:07,590 --> 00:09:10,650
For his low back pain, we
can give him some hamstrings,

164
00:09:10,650 --> 00:09:14,190
psoas, quadratus lumborum,
and paraspinal muscle

165
00:09:14,190 --> 00:09:18,750
stretches. Tell them to
do them twice a day. I

166
00:09:18,750 --> 00:09:22,670
like to tell the patients
to do three sets of 20

167
00:09:22,670 --> 00:09:26,470
-second stretching split up
with a few seconds of resting

168
00:09:26,470 --> 00:09:30,450
in between for a total of
one minute of stretching.

169
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:33,340
You always tell them
to do both sides so the

170
00:09:33,340 --> 00:09:36,770
patient would do both
hamstrings, both psoases, etc.

171
00:09:38,690 --> 00:09:42,590
And I usually like to tell
them to do it twice a day.

172
00:09:43,890 --> 00:09:46,950
For the muscle spasm,
you're doing the same thing.

173
00:09:47,110 --> 00:09:49,490
It all goes together, so the

174
00:09:49,490 --> 00:09:51,030
stretches go with
the low back pain.

175
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:56,130
For the somatic dysfunctions,
in the lumbar region,

176
00:09:56,130 --> 00:09:58,510
you're going to do some
soft tissue, some muscle

177
00:09:58,510 --> 00:10:01,210
energy, and some facilitated
positional release.

178
00:10:01,830 --> 00:10:03,610
For his lower extremity, you

179
00:10:03,610 --> 00:10:05,330
can do some muscle energy.

180
00:10:08,410 --> 00:10:10,570
Right now, you're
probably asking yourself,

181
00:10:10,570 --> 00:10:14,370
how do we treat the
L5 ERS right with FPR?

182
00:10:15,030 --> 00:10:17,840
I'm glad you were asking
that because basically

183
00:10:18,250 --> 00:10:21,270
what you do is you
neutralize the region,

184
00:10:21,270 --> 00:10:24,490
you compress, that's
your facilitating force,

185
00:10:24,490 --> 00:10:27,330
you put the dysfunction
into its freedoms,

186
00:10:27,710 --> 00:10:30,490
and then you basically
take them out

187
00:10:30,490 --> 00:10:32,490
of that and reassess.
I'll talk about

188
00:10:32,490 --> 00:10:34,790
it step by step.
For neutralizing,

189
00:10:35,310 --> 00:10:39,290
you place the dysfunction,

190
00:10:39,850 --> 00:10:42,510
take it out of extension, make
sure it's not an extension,

191
00:10:42,510 --> 00:10:44,810
not inflection, make
sure it's not rotated or

192
00:10:44,810 --> 00:10:47,830
side bent in any direction.
That's your neutralization.

193
00:10:48,030 --> 00:10:51,410
Then you add a compressive
force. So in this case, you

194
00:10:51,410 --> 00:10:54,510
would be compressing down.
If the patient's seated,

195
00:10:55,100 --> 00:11:00,550
what you would do is
you would compress down

196
00:11:00,550 --> 00:11:04,350
from the shoulders to
the L5 dysfunction.

197
00:11:05,090 --> 00:11:08,050
And you're putting just
enough pressure so that you

198
00:11:08,050 --> 00:11:12,710
can feel the compression
at your monitoring finger.

199
00:11:13,250 --> 00:11:15,630
Then maintaining
that compression,

200
00:11:15,630 --> 00:11:18,310
you're going to gently extend,

201
00:11:18,810 --> 00:11:22,150
rotate right, and sidebend
right, just until you feel

202
00:11:22,150 --> 00:11:27,870
motion at L5. So remember,
when you're at neutral,

203
00:11:28,070 --> 00:11:35,830
any degree off of zero
is going to be flexion or

204
00:11:35,830 --> 00:11:39,230
extension or rotation or
sidebending. So you don't need

205
00:11:39,230 --> 00:11:41,810
much once you're at neutral,
so just keep that in

206
00:11:41,810 --> 00:11:44,950
mind. So you're going to
gently guide the dysfunction

207
00:11:44,950 --> 00:11:50,630
into its freedoms and wait
three to five seconds.

208
00:11:50,850 --> 00:11:54,430
After three to five
seconds, you can take the

209
00:11:55,130 --> 00:11:59,050
compression off and just
bring the patient back to

210
00:11:59,050 --> 00:12:02,130
a regular seated position
or a regular starting

211
00:12:02,130 --> 00:12:03,710
position, whatever it
might have been. In this

212
00:12:03,710 --> 00:12:05,690
case, we said the patient
was sitting down, but

213
00:12:05,690 --> 00:12:07,850
if the patient's lying
down and you're treating

214
00:12:07,850 --> 00:12:10,470
the neck, you put the neck,
put the head back down

215
00:12:10,470 --> 00:12:13,010
on the table in a natural
position at the end.

216
00:12:13,150 --> 00:12:14,610
And then you reassess.

217
00:12:14,610 --> 00:12:16,010
So that's pretty much it.

218
00:12:16,010 --> 00:12:19,190
You remember, for the most
part, what we do, when we

219
00:12:19,190 --> 00:12:21,550
do FPR, we're doing
compression. So if you remember,

220
00:12:21,550 --> 00:12:25,730
neutralize, compress, freedoms,
and then reassess, you'll

221
00:12:25,730 --> 00:12:28,770
have a good idea of what
to do when you're treating

222
00:12:28,770 --> 00:12:32,090
something with FPR. If you
don't know the position to

223
00:12:32,090 --> 00:12:34,890
put it in because you didn't
learn it, just neutralize,

224
00:12:35,090 --> 00:12:38,170
compress, move it to the
freedoms, and you have it.

225
00:12:43,190 --> 00:12:44,070
To summarize,

226
00:12:45,030 --> 00:12:47,610
FPR is a passive,
indirect technique.

227
00:12:47,850 --> 00:12:52,630
We utilize the muscle
spindle, and the way to do

228
00:12:52,630 --> 00:12:55,930
it is to neutralize, add
your facilitating force,

229
00:12:56,110 --> 00:12:57,510
guide it to its freedoms,

230
00:12:58,050 --> 00:13:00,310
return to baseline,
and reassess. So

231
00:13:00,310 --> 00:13:02,330
it's neutralize,
compress, freedoms,

232
00:13:02,770 --> 00:13:05,710
return to baseline,
reassess. And if you don't

233
00:13:05,710 --> 00:13:07,290
like compression,
you can always add

234
00:13:07,290 --> 00:13:09,730
traction, and some techniques
you'll learn a little

235
00:13:09,730 --> 00:13:12,450
bit of a torque as a
facilitating force.

236
00:13:14,170 --> 00:13:19,590
That's it. Please don't
forget to do the evaluation.

237
00:13:19,590 --> 00:13:22,310
Go to the link at the
end of this PowerPoint

238
00:13:22,310 --> 00:13:25,910
and do the evaluation.
Thank you very much.